# Hermes Dashboard TCC Host (macOS 27)

A minimal, code-signed macOS app bundle whose only job is to give the Hermes Dashboard a **stable
bundle identity**, so macOS can attribute a TCC *Files & Folders* grant to an app instead of to a
bare interpreter.

## Why this exists

Apple's macOS 27 release notes (issue 184660124) state that a process **without a bundle ID** cannot
be granted App Data access belonging to another developer's application. That is exactly the
Dashboard's Chrome DevTools MCP path:

```
launchd -> Hermes Dashboard -> chrome-devtools-mcp (node) -> existing Chrome
```

The MCP `node` process must read `~/Library/Application Support/Google/Chrome/DevToolsActivePort`.
Its TCC responsible host was first uv's `python3.11`, then `/usr/bin/osascript` after the wrapper
repair. Both are bundle-less, so the **Google Chrome toggle in System Settings appears and then
silently reverts**, and `list_pages` keeps failing with `EPERM` presented as a missing file.

This bundle is the fix: a real bundle identifier plus a real code signature, registered through
Apple's own `SMAppService` LaunchAgent API.

## Layout

```
HermesDashboardHost.app/Contents/
    Info.plist                              CFBundleIdentifier + LSUIElement
    MacOS/hermes-dashboard-host             signed executable
    Library/LaunchAgents/
        ai.hermes.dashboard.host.plist      SMAppService-embedded LaunchAgent
```

The LaunchAgent uses `BundleProgram` (app-relative, SMAppService-only) plus
`BundleProgramArguments`, so launchd spawns the **signed app**, and the app `execv`s the Dashboard in
place. The chain stays `app -> Dashboard python -> node (MCP)`, and the app remains the responsible
process for every descendant through daemonization and restarts.

## Build

```bash
$HERMES_PYTHON macos/dashboard-host/build_host.py \
  --output /path/to/HermesDashboardHost.app \
  --identity "Apple Development: <REDACTED_EMAIL> (55H59PZG2Z)" \
  --child-executable "$HERMES_ROOT/venv/bin/hermes" \
  --child-arg dashboard --child-arg=--port --child-arg 9119 \
  --child-arg=--host --child-arg 127.0.0.1 --child-arg=--no-open \
  --stdout-path "$HERMES_HOME/logs/dashboard.log" \
  --stderr-path "$HERMES_HOME/logs/dashboard.error.log" \
  --working-directory "$HOME" \
  --env PATH=/usr/bin:/bin --env VIRTUAL_ENV="$HERMES_ROOT/venv" --env HERMES_HOME="$HERMES_HOME"
```

## Boundaries

- **Does not** implement a second Dashboard, Hermes, Gateway or browser framework.
- **Does not** touch `TCC.db`, Full Disk Access, Chrome, profiles/cookies, or the Gateway.
- **Does not** call `launchctl`. Building produces an artifact; lifecycle is the activation script's
  job, and the two are reviewed as separate units.
- **Does not** register itself on build. `--register` / `--unregister` / `--status` are explicit modes.

## Modes

| Mode | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `--status` | Report bundle id and service status (verifiable fields only) |
| `--register` | Register the embedded agent with `SMAppService` |
| `--unregister` | Unregister it |
| `--show-agent` | Print the resolved embedded plist path |
| `--child-exec -- <argv>` | What launchd runs: validate then `execv` the Dashboard |

`--child-exec` accepts only a Hermes entrypoint (`venv/bin/hermes`, or `python -m hermes_cli.main`)
followed by `dashboard`/`serve` and an explicit option allowlist. Unknown options, lifecycle flags,
positional arguments, relative paths and non-Hermes executables are refused, so an edited plist
cannot turn the signed app into a general-purpose launcher.

## Status honesty

`SMAppService.status` is **not** sufficient evidence of registration — an unindexed bundle can report
a non-`NotFound` status with nothing registered. Verify with `sfltool dumpbtm`, the launchd job's
program path, and the live TCC responsible process instead.
